  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 256 PM MDT Sat Aug 8 2026\n\nThis long-term forecast period will continue a suppressed ridge \npattern. The 500mb jet keeps stationed over the PNW and into the \nRockies and high plains areas of Montana through northern Wyoming. \nPrecipitation chances increase as the week goes on, with PWAT values \nincreasing daily. Wednesday through Friday looks to be the best \nchances of precipitation. Severe weather does not appear to be a \nthreat at this time in southeast Wyoming due to mostly light \nwesterly flow in the mid and upper levels. Nebraska on the other \nhand has better chances of severe weather. As the ridge pushes south \nand suppresses, faster flow aloft moves in. Wind shear values look \nto be around 30-40 knots which have the potential to produce \nmulticellular storms. Warm conditions continue through most of \nthis long-term period, but with those precipitation chances mid \n- end week, temperatures likely will likely be lower than \nearlier in the week. 700mb temperatures show ranges from the \nteens to mid-teens, supporting a cooler end of the week. Fire \nweather will continue to be a concern through Monday with low \nRHs and some gusty winds. With precipitable water, RHs, RH \nrecoveries, and precipitation chances increasing throughout the \nweek, fire weather concerns fade away.",
